00:11:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand the anxiety underneath that—the worry that if one concern gets labelled as bigotry, you'll lose the right to speak up about anything. That's a real fear, and it's worth addressing directly. But here's the distinction: parents absolutely *should* have a say in their children's education. You can and should raise concerns about curriculum, teaching methods, bullying policies, mental health support, school trips, SEN provision—legitimate issues grounded in your child's specific needs and wellbeing. Those conversations are not only welcome, they're essential. What you can't do—and what no parent in a fair society gets to do—is demand that a specific staff member be removed from a role based purely on a protected characteristic like gender identity. That's not a "safety preference," because the preference isn't based on any actual evidence of unsafety. It's based on a prejudice you hold against the group she belongs to. That's the line, and it's an important one. If a parent said, "I have a simple safety preference: I don't want my child taught by a Black teacher," or "I don't want a gay teaching assistant near my child," the school would rightly reject that—not because they're shutting down all parental input, but because that particular request is discriminatory. This situation is no different in principle. The fact that you feel strongly doesn't make the request reasonable. And letting discriminatory "preferences" dictate staffing decisions wouldn't make children safer. It would make schools less fair, less inclusive, and less safe for everyone—including children who may one day realise they are transgender themselves, or who have transgender family members. The bottom line is: your voice as a parent matters. But it doesn't entitle you to exclude an innocent person from her job based on who she is. If you can't see that, I've done all I can here. I genuinely hope you reflect on it. Goodbye.
